---
date added: 2026-10-30
title: Senate Bean Soup
author: Sarah DiGregorio
source: https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/793197025-senate-bean-soup
tags: soup, beans, ham hock, navy bean, one pot, budget, great leftovers
servings: 6
time required: 1 hour 45 minutes
cook time: 1 hour 30 minutes
notes: Senate Bean Soup has been served to senators and their staff for more than 100 years in the U.S. Senate Dining Room. This stovetop recipe honors the original — navy beans simmered with ham hocks, butter and onion — but perks it up with carrots and smoked paprika. If you'd like more vegetables, stir in about 8 ounces of chopped kale before serving.
---

Melt the @butter{1%tablespoon} in a large #soup pot{} or Dutch oven over medium-high heat. Add the @red or yellow onion{1}(finely chopped) and cook, stirring often, until the onion is softened, ~{5-6%minutes}. Add the @garlic cloves{5}(smashed and chopped) and @carrots{3%medium}(peeled and sliced ½- to 1-inch thick), and cook, stirring, until the garlic is fragrant and softened, about ~{2%minutes}. Add the @kosher salt{1%tablespoon}(such as Diamond Crystal), @smoked paprika{1.5%teaspoons}, @dried thyme{1%teaspoon} and several generous grinds of @black pepper{}; stir to combine.

Add the @water{8%cups}, @dried navy beans or small white beans{1%pound}(soaked), @smoked ham hocks{2%medium}(about 1½ pounds total) and @russet potato{1%medium}(peeled and quartered). Bring to a boil, then reduce the heat to maintain a low simmer and cover the pot. Simmer until the beans are getting close to tender, about ~{1%hour}.

Uncover the pot and simmer the soup until the beans and ham hocks are tender, the liquid level slightly reduced and the beans are a bit more prominent, ~{20-30%minutes} more.

Pull out the ham hocks and put them on a #cutting board{} to cool slightly. Using a fork, mash the potato pieces against the side of the pot. Stir vigorously to break down some of the beans, too. Stir in the @cider vinegar{1%tablespoon}. Pull the meat off the bones, add it back into the soup and discard the bones and skin. Taste and add more salt or pepper as needed.
